1. The Setup (Cells 1 to 3)What it does: The notebook first loads a configuration file named dnt_market.json. It then initializes the DntMarket object using the C++ bindings we compiled earlier.  Why it matters: It calculates exactly how many microseconds exist between the start year (2010) and end year (2026) to prepare for the massive data generation.  

2. The Heavy Lifting / The Screenshot (Cells 4 & 5)What it does: This is the exact code from your screenshot. It loops through the market.stream() in batches of 10,000 ticks. Instead of keeping everything in memory, it instantly writes each batch to your hard drive as a compressed Parquet file (dnt_python_full_ticks.parquet).  Why it matters: Generating over 84 million ticks would instantly crash your computer's RAM if it tried to hold it all at once. This streaming approach is standard big-data engineering.  

3. The OHLCV Aggregation (Cell 6)What it does: Nobody trades on microsecond data unless they are doing high-frequency trading. This cell reads the massive Parquet file and groups the millions of tiny ticks into hourly periods.  OHLCV: For every hour, it calculates the Open, High, Low, and Close prices, plus the total Volume.  The Secret Data: Notice that it also extracts a column called regime and jump_count. The regime is the actual Hidden Markov Model (HMM) state (e.g., Bull, Bear, Crash) that the C++ engine used to generate the price! It saves all this to a lightweight file named dnt_python_1h_ohlcv.csv.  

4. The Visualization (Cell 7)What it does: Finally, it uses matplotlib to draw a chart of the DNT asset's hourly close prices from 2010 to 2026.  
